# title | category | tags | body | distinct replies separated by ~
DISCUSSIONS = '''My React form loses a field when I go back a step|frontend|react,forms|I split a registration form into three components. Going back to step one clears the inputs. Should the parent own all of this state?|If the step unmounts, its local state goes away. Keeping the draft in the parent is a reasonable start.~We used one form object in the parent and passed each step only the fields it needed.~Check whether your step has a changing key too. That caused a reset in mine.~That was it, the key included the current timestamp. Removed it and the draft stays now.
Does this filter need useEffect at all?|frontend|react,state|I have a list and a search input. I'm using an effect to put matching items into another state variable. It works, but feels like extra work.|You can usually calculate filteredItems from the list and query during render.~I did this too. Removing the second state fixed a one-frame stale result.~Save effects for synchronising with something outside React. Filtering an array isn't that.~Would you add useMemo for 40 items?~Probably not until profiling shows a problem.
How do you test keyboard navigation without a mouse?|frontend|accessibility,testing|Trying to check our club website before the demo. I can tab through it, but I'm not sure what counts as broken.|Start at the address bar and tab through the page. You should always see where focus is.~Check that dialogs return focus to the button that opened them.~Try Shift+Tab as well. Our menu only worked in one direction.~Don't stop at tab order: make sure the focused control has an understandable name.
Our Django query count climbs with every card|backend|django,databases|The project list works, but the debug toolbar shows another query for each author's name. Is select_related the right fix?|For a single author foreign key, yes, select_related is the first thing I'd try.~Use prefetch_related for the team members if that's a many-to-many relationship.~Measure it again after the change. It is easy to fix authors and still fetch each avatar separately.~Got it down from 42 to 4 queries. The tags were the other part.
Should an empty search return everything?|product|search,ux|In our resource browser an empty search currently returns zero results. A teammate thinks it should show recent items instead. What would you expect?|I'd expect the normal browse view when I clear the input.~Depends whether it's a dedicated search dialog or the main list page.~We show all items but keep any category filters. Clearing text shouldn't reset those.~Good point about keeping filters. I'll test that version.
PostgreSQL dates are making my deadline move a day|backend|postgresql,timezones|A deadline entered as 2026-10-02 shows as October 1 for one teammate. It's a date, not a meeting time. What should we store?|For a day-only deadline use a date column and avoid converting midnight UTC into local time.~We had this bug when calling new Date on a date-only string and formatting it locally.~Keep timestamps for events with a time. Deadlines that mean a calendar day are a different thing.~Thank you. Separating the two removed a lot of awkward timezone code.
What goes in the README when the project is unfinished?|career|portfolio,documentation|Two screens work and two don't. I want to share the repo for feedback without making it sound finished.|Put a short working/not-yet-working list near the top.~A setup command and one screenshot help more than a huge feature wishlist.~Include the known rough edges. It makes it easier for someone to give useful feedback.~I added a small next steps section and labelled the screenshot as a prototype.
Need a second pair of eyes on our database roles|backend|security,postgresql|We have owners, members and visitors. Should the API be the only place checking who can edit a project?|If the browser talks directly to the database, database policies need to enforce it too.~Write down the permission matrix first. I found a missing rule for removing the last owner.~Test with two accounts, not just the owner account.~Also try changing the owner_id in the request body. The UI hiding that field isn't enough.
How small can a useful first open-source contribution be?|career|open-source,git|I found a typo in setup instructions but it feels too small to send a pull request for.|A setup fix is useful if it stops the next person getting stuck.~Check the contribution guide, then send a focused change explaining what was confusing.~My first merged change was a broken link. Small is fine.~Please don't bundle a whole formatting rewrite with it. Maintainers appreciate a narrow diff.
Anyone else stuck choosing between two project ideas?|community|projects,planning|One idea is more interesting, the other is something I could actually finish this term. Three people on the team and exams in six weeks.|I'd pick the one you can test with a real person this week.~Can you cut the interesting idea down to one useful workflow?~We wrote down what we could demo in ten days. That made the choice less abstract.~Not everything has to become a portfolio centrepiece. Finishing a small thing taught us plenty.
Our survey has 23 responses. Is that enough to show a chart?|product|research,data|It's for a class presentation, not a published study. I don't want a neat chart to make the result look more certain than it is.|Show the sample size and how you recruited people.~I'd show counts alongside percentages. With 23 responses the percentages look more precise than they are.~Mention who didn't respond too, if you know. Our survey only reached students already in the club.~A chart can still help describe your sample. Just avoid generalising to every student.
Flutter list jumps after I load older items|frontend|flutter,mobile|Building a small notes app. When older notes load at the top, the screen jumps and I lose my place.|Try preserving the visible item's position instead of scrolling to an absolute offset.~Stable keys on list items helped in our prototype.~Are you recreating the scroll controller on every build?~It was the controller. Moving it into the state object fixed most of the jump.
What does a good bug report look like for a class team?|community|testing,collaboration|Our group chat is full of 'it doesn't work'. I want a small template that people will actually use.|Expected result, actual result, and the shortest steps to reproduce.~Add browser/device if it's a UI problem.~A short screen recording helps, but don't make a video mandatory.~Please include the input that triggered it. 'Search is broken' took us an hour to reproduce.
Can we skip a login screen for the first prototype?|product|prototyping,scope|The core idea is a calculator. We're spending more time on accounts than the calculation itself.|If saving personal data isn't the point, a local prototype might be enough.~We used example inputs and no accounts for the first user test.~Just don't collect real private data in a prototype without thinking through storage.~Agreed, but write down that sessions and permissions are not tested yet.
Git says both of us changed the same file|backend|git,collaboration|First group project. I have a merge conflict in our routes file and I'm scared I'll erase my teammate's work.|Make a branch or commit your current work before resolving it.~Read both changes with your teammate. The correct result might include parts of both.~Don't just choose 'accept incoming' for every conflict. Run the app afterwards.~We resolved it on a call. Much less scary once we understood why both routes were needed.
Is it okay to use a simple baseline for an ML project?|backend|machine-learning,research|Our classifier barely beats a majority-class baseline. The deadline is next week. I'm not sure what to write in the report.|That comparison is a result. Explain the dataset and where the model fails.~Check class imbalance and split leakage before tuning more parameters.~A clear error analysis is more useful than hiding a weak baseline.~Keep the test set untouched while you try changes.
Figma prototype looks fine until someone uses a longer name|product|figma,design|Our cards were designed with very short labels. Now they look cramped with real content. Any quick way to stress test this?|Put the longest plausible label in the component and test at the narrowest width.~Try empty content too, not just long content.~Use auto layout and decide which text can wrap. Ellipsis everywhere hides useful information.~We made a small fixture page with long names and missing photos.
How do you ask for help without pasting the whole project?|community|learning,debugging|I keep writing massive questions and deleting them. What details do people actually need?|The smallest snippet that still reproduces the problem, plus the exact error.~Say what you expected and what you already tried.~Sometimes making the small example solves it. Annoying but true.~A repo link is okay as extra context, but don't make people search for the broken part.
Our stock count can go negative. Prevent it or record it?|product|business,data-modeling|Helping a small shop with a class prototype. A sale entered late can make the count negative even when it was a real sale.|Don't silently change the count to zero. That hides a reconciliation problem.~I'd record the correction and show a warning, but ask the shop how they handle it now.~We separated the transaction ledger from the displayed stock estimate.~Good reminder to ask about the workflow before inventing a rule.
Can a chart use colour if it also has labels?|frontend|accessibility,charts|We have three lines and direct labels at the end. Do we still need different dash styles?|Colour can help, but the labels need to remain usable where lines cross.~Try printing it in grayscale and zooming to 200 percent.~A data table is useful for readers who can't use the chart at all.~Dash styles helped our two similar blue lines. We kept the direct labels too.
Looking for a study buddy for SQL joins|community|sql,learning|I'm comfortable with SELECT and WHERE but joins still take me ages. Anyone interested in doing a few tiny examples together this weekend?|Try drawing the two tables before writing the query.~I'm working on the same thing. A small book-and-author dataset made it click for me.~Start with an inner join, then ask which unmatched rows a left join keeps.~Don't rush into five-table examples. Two tables can teach a lot.
How do you handle a teammate disappearing near the deadline?|community|teamwork,planning|They were active at the start but haven't replied this week. I don't want to assume they're being lazy.|Send a private check-in first. There may be something going on.~Make the remaining work visible so the rest of the team can plan without guessing.~Agree on a fallback date rather than sending repeated messages every few hours.~We reduced the scope and kept a record of what each person could realistically finish.
Do portfolio screenshots need fake activity numbers?|career|portfolio,ethics|The empty version of my dashboard looks less impressive, but random big numbers feel wrong. What do you usually show?|Use a clearly labelled demo dataset with relationships that actually make sense.~Show an empty state too. Handling a new account is part of the product.~A small believable example tells me more than a screenshot with 50,000 users.~Don't imply those are real customers if they're test profiles.
Deploying a student project without leaving secrets in Git|backend|security,deployment|I noticed an API key in our first commit. I removed the file in a new commit. Is that enough?|Rotate the key. Deleting it from the latest file doesn't remove it from history.~Keep only public configuration in the browser bundle.~Add an example env file with placeholder names, not real values.~We also checked CI logs because the key had been printed during a failed build.
Our model is good on train and bad on validation|backend|machine-learning,python|It's a small image dataset for a class assignment. Accuracy looks great on train but drops a lot on a separate folder.|Check that preprocessing is the same for both sets.~Look at a few actual mistakes instead of only the accuracy number.~The training data might be too narrow. Are all images taken against the same background?~Yes, almost all training photos are on one desk. We'll document that limitation.
Do you write tests before the first prototype?|backend|testing,projects|We have a tiny expense-splitting prototype. Trying to decide what deserves tests this early.|Test the rounding and split calculation. Those are easy to get subtly wrong.~I wouldn't test every CSS class. Focus on the behaviour you could break without noticing.~Add a regression test when you fix a bug that might return.~For money-like values, include a case where the split doesn't divide evenly.
What's a useful question to ask after a usability test?|product|user-research,ux|I keep asking 'was it easy?' and everyone says yes even after getting stuck.|Ask what they expected to happen at the point they paused.~Watch the task first, then ask about specific moments rather than a general rating.~Avoid explaining the interface while they're trying it. I kept doing that by accident.~We ask what they would do next without showing the next screen.
Trying to make a CSV import less fragile|backend|data,python|Our import fails when a description contains a comma or a line break. Is there a better separator?|Use a CSV parser that handles quoted fields. A different separator won't solve every case.~Test quotes inside quoted text too.~Keep an import report with row numbers so someone can fix a bad record.~We were splitting on commas by hand. Replaced that with the standard csv module.
How much detail should a task description have?|community|projects,teamwork|Our board has tasks like 'backend' and nobody knows when they're done. I want to improve it without writing an essay per card.|Write one observable outcome and how to check it.~'Save a draft and restore it after reload' is easier to finish than 'do frontend'.~Add dependencies if another task genuinely blocks it.~We now include a small done checklist. Two or three items is usually enough.
Can I use a university logo in a fictional demo?|product|design,portfolio|The project is a student prototype and isn't affiliated with the university. I don't want the mockup to look official.|A generic placeholder mark avoids implying endorsement.~Check the institution's published brand rules if you want to use its actual logo.~Label the prototype clearly and avoid official-looking approval badges.~We're keeping the project name separate from the institution name.
How do you keep dates readable across countries?|frontend|localization,ux|Our cards say 04/05 and half the team reads it differently. Should we force one format?|Use a month name where the space allows it.~For date-only deadlines, be careful not to apply a timezone conversion.~A full date in the tooltip is useful but shouldn't be the only readable label.~We switched to 5 Apr and included the year in details.
Anyone tried QGIS for a non-geography class project?|community|qgis,data|I have a CSV of public park locations and want to make a simple map. Most tutorials assume I already know GIS terms.|Start by checking which columns are latitude and longitude.~The coordinate reference system matters; write down what your source uses.~Keep the source link and the date you downloaded it.~A map can look convincing even with swapped coordinates. Check a few known places.
Should deleted comments disappear or leave a marker?|product|community,ux|A deleted parent comment makes replies confusing in our prototype. We haven't decided what should happen.|A marker can preserve the shape of a conversation without keeping the body.~Think about whether replies quote personal information from the deleted comment.~Whatever you choose, test deleting a parent with replies before shipping.~We need to separate removing content from removing the whole thread of replies.
I finally reproduced the double-booking bug|backend|postgresql,concurrency|Two people could reserve the same equipment slot if they clicked together. Normal manual testing never caught it.|Nice catch. Put the rule in the database transaction, not just a disabled button.~A unique constraint helps for discrete slots; ranges need a different approach.~Keep the concurrent-request test around so it doesn't come back.~We added a test that sends both requests together. One now gets a useful error.
How do you pick a first issue in a repo you don't know?|career|open-source,learning|There are lots of good-first-issue labels, but some haven't had a reply in months.|Read recent discussions to see whether someone is already working on it.~Try running the project locally before promising a fix.~A short question about the expected behaviour can save a lot of work.~Don't be discouraged if the label is stale. Maintaining those lists takes time.
Our accessibility checklist is getting too long|product|accessibility,planning|We can't finish every improvement before the class demo. How would you choose what to do first?|Fix anything that prevents completing the main task with a keyboard.~Check labels and error messages on the main form.~Keep the remaining issues visible instead of calling the whole app accessible.~We split blockers from improvements and added a person responsible for each.
Do I need Docker for a two-person Django project?|backend|django,devops|One of us is on Windows, one on Linux. Most of the trouble is setting up the database the same way.|A documented setup and pinned dependencies may solve enough for now.~Docker can help with the database, but adds another thing to learn.~We containerised only PostgreSQL first and kept the app outside it.~Whatever you choose, try the instructions on a clean machine.
What should we do with missing survey answers?|product|research,statistics|Some people skipped the last three questions. Replacing blanks with zero seems wrong but breaks my spreadsheet chart.|Keep missing distinct from an actual zero.~Report how many responses each question has.~Check whether those questions were optional or whether the form made them hard to answer.~We added a separate missing count rather than inventing values.
How do you make a short project demo less stressful?|career|presentations,projects|Five minutes, one laptop, unreliable Wi-Fi. I'm more nervous about the setup than the speaking.|Have a local screenshot or recording of the main flow as a fallback.~Demo one complete task instead of every menu item.~Make the example data ahead of time so you aren't typing long forms on stage.~Practice once with a timer. Our intro used half the slot the first time.
When is localStorage the wrong place for a draft?|frontend|browser,privacy|We want form drafts to survive reloads. The form might contain personal notes, though.|First decide whether persistence is needed and explain it to users.~Don't store passwords or tokens you handle yourself just to make a form convenient.~A per-session draft may be enough if it's a shared computer.~Also plan what happens on logout and when another account signs in.
Learning Linux permissions one error at a time|backend|linux,learning|I keep using sudo when something fails and now half my project files have a different owner. How do I untangle this?|Check who owns the files before changing permissions recursively.~Understand what the command is trying to access. Permission denied isn't always asking for sudo.~Avoid running your package manager as root inside the project.~I fixed ownership on my project directory and wrote down which command caused it.
How can a business student help on a technical team?|community|business,collaboration|I can do research and spreadsheets but I'm new to coding. I don't want to just make presentation slides.|Testing the problem with potential users is real project work.~Help the team define what success would look like before building more screens.~Good content and a clear onboarding flow are often neglected.~I joined a team by doing five interviews and turning the findings into a smaller backlog.
What does 'done' mean for a student research prototype?|research|research,scope|Our supervisor wants a working demonstration. We keep adding features because the tool doesn't feel finished.|Agree on a specific demonstration and the limitations you'll explain.~A reproducible experiment can be more valuable than a polished extra screen.~Write a small acceptance checklist with your supervisor.~We cut two features and spent that time checking the results.
Blender exports are too large for our gallery page|frontend|blender,performance|The scene looks fine on my laptop but takes forever to load on a phone. Where would you start?|Look at texture sizes before chasing tiny mesh savings.~Measure the exported file, not just the Blender project size.~Could a still image explain the project just as well on mobile?~We kept an optional 3D view and loaded a small poster image first.
How do you ask a mentor for feedback on code?|career|mentorship,review|I have a repo but I'm not sure what to ask beyond 'is this good?'.|Pick one trade-off you're unsure about and explain your current choice.~Tell them what the app is meant to do and how to run it.~A small pull request is easier to review than an entire repo.~I asked about my permission checks and got much more useful feedback than on naming every file.
Simple project ideas for learning database transactions?|backend|postgresql,learning|I understand commit and rollback on paper. Looking for a tiny example where they really matter.|Transfer a fictional balance between two accounts and make the second update fail.~Try reserving the last seat with two requests at once.~Use fake data and write the invariant before the code.~A borrowing app also works: an item can't have two active loans at once.
What would make you trust a project progress bar?|product|projects,ux|Ours counts completed tasks, but one huge task and five tiny tasks make the number misleading.|Label it 'tasks completed' rather than suggesting it measures all effort.~We show done/total next to the bar so the meaning is clear.~Don't invent precision. A milestone summary can be more honest for some projects.~We split the huge task into testable outcomes instead of adding arbitrary weights.
Phone camera photos rotate after uploading|frontend|mobile,images|The preview looks right on my phone, then sideways on the project page. Anyone seen this?|Check the image orientation metadata and whether your processing library applies it.~Try a portrait and a landscape photo from the same phone.~Keep an original for debugging instead of repeatedly saving the broken output.~That pointed us in the right direction. Our thumbnail step ignored orientation.
How do you keep a project alive during exams?|community|planning,college|We're enjoying it but none of us can promise weekly releases this month.|Pause deliberately and leave a note explaining the next step.~We agreed on one small maintenance slot and no new features until exams finish.~It's okay to archive a prototype that served its purpose.~A short handover note is surprisingly helpful when you return after a few weeks.
Can an API return a useful error without leaking details?|backend|security,apis|Right now users see the database exception text. It helped us debug but doesn't seem right for normal use.|Return a stable public message and keep detailed diagnostics server-side.~Validation errors can name the field without exposing table names or queries.~Don't put secrets or full request headers in your logs either.~We added a request identifier so we can find the error without showing the stack trace.
Our club wants a newsletter. Is a spreadsheet enough first?|product|business,scope|I'm the person building the site and everyone immediately asked for an admin dashboard. We have about thirty interested readers.|Try the manual workflow first and see what actually repeats.~Consent and unsubscribe handling matter even for a small list.~Use an existing mailing tool before writing your own sender.~We're documenting the workflow before adding another dashboard.
How do you give helpful feedback on a rough design?|community|design,feedback|My teammate asked for feedback but all I can think is 'it looks busy'. I want to be more specific.|Pick a task and explain where you weren't sure what to click.~Separate the issue from your preferred solution.~Try 'I missed the save button after editing' instead of 'make it pop'.~Asking what they want feedback on helps too. They might still be working on the layout.
SQL count is bigger after adding a join|backend|sql,databases|The project count doubles when I join members and tasks. Both tables have several rows per project. What am I missing?|You're counting combinations from both joins. Aggregate separately or count the distinct thing you mean.~Draw one project with two members and three tasks; the joined result has six rows.~Be careful with COUNT DISTINCT as a blanket fix. Check the other aggregates too.~The six-row example made it click. I moved each count into its own subquery.
Anyone learning technical writing alongside programming?|community|writing,learning|I can make the code work but explaining the setup is taking longer than I expected.|Ask someone to follow your instructions without coaching them.~Write down prerequisites and what successful output looks like.~Short sections with exact commands beat a long introduction.~I keep a list of every step I forgot on a fresh install.
What should an offline app do when two edits disagree?|backend|mobile,sync|Our field notes app can be edited on two devices. Last-write-wins is easy, but I don't want to silently lose notes.|Keep both versions when the text differs and let the user choose.~Separate fields that can merge safely from fields that need a decision.~Start by making the conflict visible. Automatic merging can come later.~We added a small 'needs review' state with both timestamps.
How do you explain a project that didn't meet its goal?|career|portfolio,reflection|Our prototype worked technically but nobody wanted to use it twice. Can that still go in a portfolio?|Yes, if you explain what you learned and what evidence changed your mind.~Show the original assumption and how you tested it.~Avoid rewriting the story as a success. Honest reflection is useful.~We're including the interview notes and the scope we'd change next time.
Need ideas for testing a map without asking people to travel|product|maps,research|Our student map prototype has only a few locations. We want feedback on the interface before collecting more data.|Give someone a scenario and use a small set of clearly labelled sample locations.~Test the list view too. Not everyone wants to navigate with a map.~Ask what information they'd need before deciding to go there.~We discovered opening hours mattered more than the marker colours.
Python virtual environments finally make sense|community|python,learning|Small win: rebuilt my project on another laptop using the requirements file. Last month I copied the whole environment folder.|Nice! Try documenting the Python version as well.~A clean install is a good test of your setup notes.~Keep local env folders out of Git; the dependency file is what you share.~Next thing I'm trying is running that setup check in CI.
Should notifications link to a comment or the whole discussion?|product|notifications,ux|We show 'new reply' but the link lands at the top of a long discussion. Is that good enough for a first release?|At least include the discussion title so the user knows where they landed.~If you can, link to the specific reply and highlight it briefly.~Think about what the link does if the comment was removed.~We added a fallback to the discussion when the reply no longer exists.
How do you decide which skills to put on a profile?|career|skills,portfolio|I've used several tools once in class. Listing all of them as advanced feels wrong.|List what you can discuss and mark your current level honestly.~A project showing how you used a tool helps more than a huge skills list.~Keep 'learning' separate from things you can help someone with.~I removed a few tools and linked the projects where I actually used the rest.
Is a paused project the same thing as a failed project?|community|projects,college|We paused ours because two teammates started placements. It still has useful work in it.|No. Leave a clear status and a note about what remains.~Archive it if nobody expects to return soon, but keep the write-up.~A working small piece can still help the next team.~We recorded a two-minute walkthrough before pausing. Glad we did.'''
